ceml_version: "0.1"
circuit_id: "rlc_series"
description: "Single-loop series RLC circuit driven by a voltage source generator — KNOWN LIMIT CASE, see notes below"

# NOTE (kept intentionally, do not "fix" by adding R/L/C to find or giving them values):
# The source question gives only |VR|, |VL|, |VC| (Vac across each element) — no A, no
# frequency, no phase. From that data alone, R, L and C are NOT individually determinable:
# 5 unknowns (I, omega, R, L, C) vs. 3 equations (VR=I*R, VL=I*omega*L, VC=I/(omega*C)).
# What IS solvable from |VR|,|VL|,|VC| alone is Vs (amplitude), via the phasor voltage
# triangle: Vs = VR + j(VL - VC) (KVL, phasor sum — NOT a scalar sum of magnitudes).
#
# R, L and C are deliberately left with no `value` and NOT listed in `find`. Per spec
# section 9 this is a fatal error ("value absent from component not listed in find") —
# that failure is the point of this example, not a bug to route around. A component
# without a value that we're also not asking to find is out of AI.ciOne's scope: AI.ciOne
# solves well-posed problems that converge to a definite result, it does not perform
# open-ended feasibility/justification analysis ("is R computable? justify") even though
# real exam questions sometimes ask exactly that. CEML's job is only to document the
# circuit; it makes no claim that every documented circuit is solvable.

nodes:
    - id: GND
      type: ground
    - id: N1
    - id: N2
    - id: N3

components:
    - id: Vs
      type: voltage_source
      regime: AC
      pins: {p: N1, n: GND}
    - id: R
      type: resistor
      pins: [N1, N2]
    - id: L
      type: inductor
      pins: [N2, N3]
    - id: C
      type: capacitor
      pins: [N3, GND]

specs:
  given:
    - Vac(N1, N2): 6
    - Vac(N2, N3): 2
    - Vac(N3, GND): 10
  find:
    - Vs
